    <string name="settings_key_hint">Key hint</string>
    <string name="settings_language_note">The language button cycles through the ones you tick, in this order.</string>
    <string name="settings_language_minimum">At least one language has to stay on.</string>
    <string name="settings_buttons_note">Remotes disagree about which buttons exist and about what they report. Press the button you want and it gets recorded. Deleting and digits work without any of this; switching language needs a button of its own.</string>
    <string name="settings_binding_row">%1$s: %2$s</string>
    <string name="settings_binding_unset">not set</string>
    <string name="settings_system_keyboard">Android keyboard settings</string>
    <string name="settings_system_keyboard_note">Leaves this app. Enable or switch the active keyboard there.</string>
    <string name="settings_check_updates">Check for updates</string>
    <string name="settings_updates_note">The only thing here that uses the network. It runs in a separate process, only when you press it, and sends nothing. What you type never leaves the device.</string>
    <string name="settings_typing_note">2-9 pick a letter group. 0 is space. 1 is punctuation, and pressing it again cycles the marks. Up and down walk the alternatives, and so do channel up and down. Left and right move the caret. Holding left deletes. Centre submits, back closes.\n\nAccept is optional: pressing the next group key accepts the previous letter on its own.\n\nThe digit printed on 2-9 is the last alternative on that key, and the walk wraps, so one press of down types it — enough for a digit inside a word. A field that asks for numbers opens in digit mode instead, and the button above switches that mode by hand for a longer run.</string>
    <string name="settings_scratch_hint">Type here</string>
    <string name="settings_scratch_note">Focus this field to bring the keyboard up. Nothing here is saved.</string>

